    "prompt": "You are extracting the numeric claims of one section of a model-validation report, so that each\ncan be checked against the artifact it cites. Section: outcomes.\n\nThe prose is printed below one line per line, each prefixed with its own number and a vertical\nbar. List EVERY number that appears in the prose, in the order it appears, including numbers that\nappear inside a citation's logical name and numbers written with a thousands separator or a per\ncent sign. For each one:\n\n- \"line\" is the number printed to the left of the line the number appears on. Give the number\n  only; do not copy the line's text back, and do not count the prefix itself as a number.\n- \"value\" is the number as written, with thousands separators and the per cent sign removed:\n  \"3,500\" is 3500, \"22.0%\" is 22.0.\n- \"unit\" is \"percent\" when the number is written with a per cent sign, \"count\" for a number of\n  rows, features or events, \"currency\", \"bp\" for basis points, \"months\" for a horizon, and\n  \"ratio\" for every other dimensionless quantity (AUC, PSI, VIF, a coefficient, a lift).\n- \"metric\" is what the number measures, taken from the logical name of the artifact it cites\n  (auc, brier, psi, vif, calibration_slope, delta_auc, coefficient, event_rate, ...), or null.\n- \"split\" is train, test, out_of_time or vintage_holdout, or null.\n- \"comparison\" is \"eq\" for a number that equals its artifact; \"delta\" for a difference written\n  \"from a to b\" or \"a gap of x\", whose value is the second cited artifact minus the first; and\n  \"ratio\" for a number written as one artifact divided by another. A delta or ratio carries TWO\n  adjacent citations, in that order.\n- \"citation\" is the [[art:...]] token the number carries, copied exactly, or null when the\n  sentence carries none. Do not invent a citation and do not move one from another number.\n- \"rounding\" is the number of decimals the prose wrote when it wrote fewer than the artifact has\n  (2 for a lift written 2.24), or null.\n\nReturn every number even when it has no citation: a number you leave out is still counted, and\ncounted against the report.\n\nProse:\n 1| | Split | n | Loans | Event rate | Mean pred. | AUC | Gini | KS | Brier | LogLoss | Cal. slope | CPR actual | CPR pred. |\n 2| |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n 3| | train | 36,474 | 1,395 | 0.008609 | 0.008694 | 0.7888 | 0.5775 | 0.4561 | 0.008396 | 0.04442 | 0.996 | 9.86% | 9.95% |\n 4| | test | 15,382 | 400 | 0.008061 | 0.008364 | 0.7759 | 0.5519 | 0.4195 | 0.007893 | 0.04261 | 0.939 | 9.26% | 9.59% |\n 5| | vintage_holdout | 32,177 | 666 | 0.004817 | 0.005914 | 0.7722 | 0.5443 | 0.4492 | 0.004765 | 0.02814 | 0.836 | 5.63% | 6.87% |\n 6| | out_of_time | 12,257 | 515 | 0.017949 | 0.019451 | 0.7871 | 0.5742 | 0.4422 | 0.017122 | 0.07960 | 1.008 | 19.53% | 21.00% |\n 7| \n 8| **Discrimination is adequate and stable.** AUC ranges from 0.7722 to 0.7888 across all four populations. The train-to-test gap is 0.0128 and the train-to-vintage-holdout gap is 0.0166; out of time, AUC is essentially unchanged from train at 0.7871. These gaps are well inside any conventional degradation threshold, and KS is stable in a 0.42–0.46 band. There is no evidence of overfitting in the ranking dimension and no O1 finding is raised. Brier and log-loss differences across splits are driven almost entirely by differences in base rate and carry no independent signal.\n 9| \n10| **Calibration is the problem, and it is directional.** The model over-predicts in all four splits, without exception, and the size of the over-prediction increases monotonically with distance from the training regime: +1.0% in train (0.008694 vs 0.008609), +3.8% in test (0.008364 vs 0.008061), +8.4% out of time (0.019451 vs 0.017949), and +22.8% on the vintage holdout (0.005914 vs 0.004817). In the units that matter for valuation, the vintage holdout is predicted at 6.87 CPR against 5.63 realized — 124 basis points of CPR, 22% in relative terms — and the out-of-time split at 21.00 against 19.53, a further 146 basis points. A bias that never changes sign across four independent populations is not sampling noise; it is a level error in the fitted hazard.\n11| \n12| The calibration slope tells the same story from the other direction, declining from 0.996 in train to 0.939 in test and 0.836 on the vintage holdout. A slope of 0.836 means predicted log-odds are roughly 16% too dispersed relative to realized outcomes on that population: the model is not only too high on average there, it is too confident in the spread of its rankings. Notably, the out-of-time slope of 1.008 is fine, so the slope deterioration is specific to the seasoned/burned-out vintage-holdout population rather than being a general property of the fit. The most likely explanation, consistent with the rows-per-loan asymmetry noted in section 3, is that burnout is under-captured: the model does not decay the hazard fast enough for loans that have already passed through refinance opportunities without acting.\n13| \n14| For MSR use, the direction is conservative for asset value — over-predicting CPR shortens projected life and understates MSR fair value — but a 22% relative CPR error is material regardless of sign, and the same bias applied to a hedging or amortization decision is not conservative at all. These are recorded as findings C1-01 (high) and C1-02 (medium).\n\n\nAnswer with one JSON object and nothing else: no prose before or after it, no explanation, no markdown fence. It must validate against this JSON schema:\n{\"$defs\": {\"Comparison\": {\"description\": \"What the claimed value is asserted to equal.\\n\\n``delta`` and ``ratio`` are cited by two adjacent ``[[art:...]]`` tokens, in order (D-012).\\nThe enum is not nullable: ``eq`` is the explicit default, so \\\"this number equals its artifact\\\"\\nhas one spelling and not two (D-018).\\n\\nAttributes:\\n    eq: The value matches the cited artifact.\\n    delta: The value matches ``second - first``.\\n    ratio: The value matches ``second / first``.\", \"enum\": [\"eq\", \"delta\", \"ratio\"], \"title\": \"Comparison\", \"type\": \"string\"}, \"ExtractedClaim\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"description\": \"One claim as the model returns it: the grammar minus the fields the caller already knows.\\n\\n``text``, ``section``, ``id`` and ``source`` are not asked for. The caller knows which section\\nit passed in, the id is a hash of the other three fields, the source of an extraction is\\nalways ``report``, and the text is the line the model named -- which the caller has in front\\nof it, so asking for it back costs output tokens and adds a way for the answer to disagree\\nwith the prose (D-063, D-085).\\n\\nAttributes:\\n    line: The 1-based number of the line the number appears on, as the prompt printed it.\\n    value: The number as written, separators and per cent sign removed.\\n    unit: How it is written.\\n    metric: What it measures, or ``None``.\\n    split: Which split it is about, or ``None``.\\n    comparison: ``eq``, ``delta`` or ``ratio``.\\n    citation: The citation the number carries, or ``None``.\\n    rounding: Decimals the prose used, or ``None``.\", \"properties\": {\"line\": {\"minimum\": 1, \"title\": \"Line\", \"type\": \"integer\"}, \"value\": {\"title\": \"Value\", \"type\": \"number\"}, \"unit\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/Unit\", \"default\": \"ratio\"}, \"metric\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"string\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null, \"title\": \"Metric\"}, \"split\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/SplitName\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null}, \"comparison\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/Comparison\", \"default\": \"eq\"}, \"citation\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"string\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null, \"title\": \"Citation\"}, \"rounding\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"minimum\": 0, \"type\": \"integer\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null, \"title\": \"Rounding\"}}, \"required\": [\"line\", \"value\"], \"title\": \"ExtractedClaim\", \"type\": \"object\"}, \"SplitName\": {\"description\": \"The splits a claim may be stated on, exactly as ``CLAIMS_SCHEMA.json`` enumerates them.\\n\\nAttributes:\\n    train: The development split.\\n    test: The held-out split.\\n    out_of_time: A later-period split; hazard subjects.\\n    vintage_holdout: One origination year held out; hazard subjects.\", \"enum\": [\"train\", \"test\", \"out_of_time\", \"vintage_holdout\"], \"title\": \"SplitName\", \"type\": \"string\"}, \"Unit\": {\"description\": \"How a number is written, which decides how it is normalised before matching.\\n\\nAttributes:\\n    ratio: Every dimensionless quantity not written with a ``%`` -- AUC, PSI, VIF, a\\n        coefficient, a lift.\\n    percent: Written with a ``%``; normalised against a unit-interval artifact by dividing\\n        by 100.\\n    count: A number of rows, features or events. Matched to half a unit, so integers match\\n        exactly.\\n    currency: A money amount.\\n    bp: Basis points, as a rate shock is written.\\n    months: A horizon.\", \"enum\": [\"ratio\", \"percent\", \"count\", \"currency\", \"bp\", \"months\"], \"title\": \"Unit\", \"type\": \"string\"}}, \"additionalProperties\": false, \"description\": \"The JSON object the extractor is asked for: a list of claims under one key.\\n\\nA bare JSON array is a shape more models get wrong than a single-key object, and\\n:func:`~quaestor.llm.structured.structured` validates a ``BaseModel``, not a list.\\n\\nAttributes:\\n    claims: The claims, in the order the numbers appear.\", \"properties\": {\"claims\": {\"items\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/ExtractedClaim\"}, \"title\": \"Claims\", \"type\": \"array\"}}, \"title\": \"ExtractedClaims\", \"type\": \"object\"}",
